    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d685">

                <pb facs="0636" n="629" sameAs="frus:frus1937v03/pg629"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94111/82: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Bullitt</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-10-21T15:00:00+00:00">October 21, 1937—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received October 21—12:30 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1482. Reference Embassy’s 1468 to 1470, October 19, 4 p.m. The Chinese Ambassador
                    states in confidence that he saw Delbos<anchor n="49" xml:id="d685-f1"/>
                    yesterday regarding the prohibition of transit shipments through Indo-China, and
                    pressed for a reconsideration of the matter by the French Government, stating
                    that it was “a question of life and death for China”. Delbos gave Wellington Koo
                    to understand that he would ask Chautemps<anchor n="50" xml:id="d685-f2"/> to
                    have the matter considered again by the French Ministers dealing with national
                    defense.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Bullitt</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d685-f1" n="49" xml:id="d685-f1-content">
                <p>French Minister for Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d685-f2" n="50" xml:id="d685-f2-content">
